-- CISSP Domain 2 (Asset Security) expansion, part B of 2 (sibling 20260726000184_expand_cissp_d2a.sql
-- covers 2.1-2.3; not touched here). Adds flashcards ci-f-700..730, ci-a-250..260, ci-y-150..156 and
-- questions ci-q-700..741 for objective 2.4 (data roles & lifecycle management: owner/custodian/steward,
-- data remanence, NIST SP 800-88 media sanitization, disposal-oriented lifecycle roles), objective 2.5
-- (asset retention: retention schedules, legal hold/spoliation/e-discovery, records management, secure
-- disposal timing - previously zero content for this sub-objective), and objective 2.6 (data security
-- controls & compliance: data-state protection mechanisms including confidential computing/homomorphic
-- encryption, DLP placement/detection techniques, cross-border transfer mechanisms, FIPS 199-driven
-- baseline scoping/tailoring/overlays, masking/tokenization/anonymization/pseudonymization). Deliberately
-- goes beyond, rather than restating, the existing owner/custodian (ci-f-013/078/082), data-states
-- (ci-f-015/081-deleted, ci-q-011/072), Clear/Purge/Destroy (ci-f-016/019-deleted/080, ci-q-010/013/070/071),
-- DLP-definition (ci-f-018), sovereignty-definition (ci-f-020), and scoping/tailoring-definition
-- (ci-f-082, ci-q-073/074) facts already present under their current (some pre-existing-bug-mistagged) ids.

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- ---------- 2.4 data roles & lifecycle management: core ----------
('ci-f-700','cissp','core',2,'2.4',$$What is the difference between a data steward and a data custodian?$$,$$The data steward is responsible for the quality, context, and business meaning of data - defining what fields mean, ensuring accuracy and consistent use - while the data custodian carries out the technical safeguards (backups, access controls, encryption, sanitization) that the data owner directs. Stewardship is about data content and usability; custodianship is about protecting it.$$),
('ci-f-701','cissp','core',2,'2.4',$$How does a system owner's responsibility differ from a data owner's?$$,$$The system owner is accountable for the hardware/software platform itself - its operation, patching, and procurement - while the data owner is accountable for the data that platform hosts, including its classification and who may access it. A single system often holds data belonging to multiple data owners, all served by one system owner.$$),
('ci-f-702','cissp','core',2,'2.4',$$How does NIST SP 800-88 map data sensitivity to a required sanitization category?$$,$$800-88 ties the sanitization decision to the confidentiality impact of the data being removed: low-impact data may only need Clear, moderate-impact data typically requires Purge, and high-impact data being fully retired from use generally requires Destroy. The category is chosen for the exposure risk, not merely because media happens to be old.$$),
('ci-f-703','cissp','core',2,'2.4',$$Why is degaussing an unreliable sanitization method for SSDs and other flash media?$$,$$Degaussing erases data by disrupting the magnetic domains on platters, so it only works on magnetic media (HDDs, tapes). SSDs store data electrically in flash cells with no magnetic domains, so degaussing does nothing to them - and even overwrite commands are unreliable because wear-leveling and over-provisioning mean the drive silently relocates data to blocks a standard overwrite pass may never touch.$$),
('ci-f-704','cissp','core',2,'2.4',$$What is cryptographic erasure (crypto-shredding) and when is it preferred?$$,$$Crypto-shredding sanitizes data by destroying the encryption key that protects it rather than the data itself, instantly rendering the remaining ciphertext unrecoverable. It is the preferred method for self-encrypting drives, SSDs (where overwrite is unreliable), and cloud/multi-tenant storage where physical destruction of shared media isn't possible.$$),
('ci-f-705','cissp','core',2,'2.4',$$What is a certificate of sanitization/destruction and why does it matter?$$,$$A signed document (from an internal team or a third-party vendor) recording what media was sanitized, by what method, when, and by whom. It provides an auditable record that due diligence was performed, supports compliance evidence, and is the artifact regulators or auditors expect to see when reviewing an organization's disposal practices.$$),
('ci-f-706','cissp','core',2,'2.4',$$Which data lifecycle role typically acts at the Create stage versus the Destroy stage?$$,$$At Create, the data owner (or their delegate) assigns the initial classification that governs the data's handling for its entire life. At Destroy, the data custodian carries out (or oversees a vendor performing) the sanitization method the owner's classification requires, and documents that it occurred.$$),
('ci-f-707','cissp','core',2,'2.4',$$What data quality dimensions does a data steward typically manage?$$,$$Accuracy (the data reflects reality), completeness (no missing required values), consistency (the same fact agrees across systems), and timeliness (the data is current enough to be useful) - stewardship focuses on these usability properties, distinct from the custodian's security-control duties.$$),
('ci-f-708','cissp','core',2,'2.4',$$Why must sanitization be verified after it is performed, not just assumed complete?$$,$$Overwrite and purge operations can fail silently - bad sectors may be skipped, firmware may misreport success, or a script may error partway through. Verification (sampling recovered blocks, running vendor-supplied validation tools, or independent audit) confirms the data is actually unrecoverable before media is released, reused, or discarded.$$),
('ci-f-709','cissp','core',2,'2.4',$$What factors determine which sanitization method (Clear, Purge, or Destroy) an organization should choose for a given piece of media?$$,$$The media type (magnetic, flash, optical), the sensitivity/classification of the data it held, and the media's destination after sanitization (reused internally at the same level, reused/reissued outside that trust boundary, or permanently discarded) together determine the minimum acceptable category under NIST SP 800-88.$$),
-- ---------- 2.4 data roles & lifecycle management: acronym ----------
('ci-a-250','cissp','acronym',2,'2.4',$$SSD$$,$$Solid State Drive - flash-based storage with no magnetic platters; requires cryptographic erasure or vendor Secure Erase for reliable sanitization since overwriting and degaussing are unreliable due to wear-leveling.$$),
('ci-a-251','cissp','acronym',2,'2.4',$$HDD$$,$$Hard Disk Drive - magnetic platter storage; can be sanitized by overwriting (Clear), degaussing (Purge), or physical destruction (Destroy) per NIST SP 800-88.$$),
('ci-a-252','cissp','acronym',2,'2.4',$$NIST$$,$$National Institute of Standards and Technology - publishes the SP 800-88 media sanitization guidelines (Clear/Purge/Destroy) referenced throughout asset security.$$),
-- ---------- 2.4 data roles & lifecycle management: feynman ----------
('ci-y-150','cissp','feynman',2,'2.4',$$Explain why deleting a file doesn't actually remove the data, and how you decide the right way to truly get rid of it.$$,$$Model answer: Deleting a file usually just removes the pointer the file system uses to find the data - the operating system marks that space as available, but the actual bits stay on the disk until something else overwrites them. That leftover data is called remanence, and anyone with the right recovery tool can often pull it back. To truly remove it you pick a method matched to the media and the data's sensitivity: Clear (overwrite) is fine if the drive stays inside your organization at the same trust level; Purge (degauss or crypto-erase) is needed if it's leaving that trust boundary but the media will still be reused somewhere; Destroy (shred, pulverize, incinerate) is for the most sensitive data or media leaving your control entirely. The point is matching the strength of the erasure to how bad it would be if the data resurfaced.$$),
('ci-y-151','cissp','feynman',2,'2.4',$$Explain why an SSD needs a different sanitization approach than a traditional hard drive.$$,$$Model answer: A traditional hard drive stores bits as magnetic orientation on a physical platter, so overwriting a sector with new data - or degaussing the whole platter - genuinely destroys the old signal. An SSD stores bits electrically in flash cells, and its controller constantly moves data around behind the scenes (wear-leveling) to spread out wear and keep spare blocks in reserve (over-provisioning). That means when you tell an SSD to overwrite a file, the controller may write the new data to a completely different physical location and leave the old copy sitting in a reallocated block your overwrite command never touched. Because you can't reliably reach every physical location where a copy might live, the safer approach is cryptographic erasure: encrypt the drive from day one, then destroy the key. Without the key, every copy of the ciphertext anywhere on the drive - reachable or not - is useless.$$),
-- ---------- 2.5 asset retention: core ----------
('ci-f-710','cissp','core',2,'2.5',$$What is a records retention schedule?$$,$$A formal policy document that specifies, for each category of record an organization holds, how long it must be kept and what happens to it afterward (destroy, archive, or transfer). It is the governing artifact that turns legal, regulatory, and business retention requirements into concrete, enforceable actions.$$),
('ci-f-711','cissp','core',2,'2.5',$$What is a legal hold (litigation hold)?$$,$$A directive issued when litigation is reasonably anticipated or has begun, requiring the organization to preserve all records relevant to the matter - overriding the normal retention schedule and suspending any automated deletion or purge jobs that would otherwise destroy them, until the hold is formally released.$$),
('ci-f-712','cissp','core',2,'2.5',$$What is spoliation and why is it a serious risk?$$,$$Spoliation is the destruction, alteration, or failure to preserve evidence that is relevant to pending or reasonably anticipated litigation. Courts can sanction a party for it - up to an adverse inference instruction (the jury is told to assume the destroyed evidence was unfavorable), monetary penalties, or in severe cases default judgment - making legal hold compliance a serious organizational obligation, not a paperwork formality.$$),
('ci-f-713','cissp','core',2,'2.5',$$What is the difference between a retention period's regulatory minimum and its organizational maximum?$$,$$The regulatory (or contractual) minimum is the shortest time a record may legally be kept before destruction is permitted - keeping it any less risks fines or sanctions. The organizational maximum is a risk-driven ceiling the organization sets for itself - keeping data past its useful/required life only expands breach exposure and legal discovery scope with no compensating benefit, so records should generally be disposed of once the minimum is met and no other need or hold exists.$$),
('ci-f-714','cissp','core',2,'2.5',$$What does the data minimization / storage limitation principle require?$$,$$A core privacy principle (codified in GDPR Article 5) that personal data must be kept in a form permitting identification of the data subject for no longer than necessary for the purposes it was collected for. It obligates an organization to justify continued retention against an active purpose, not simply retain data indefinitely by default.$$),
('ci-f-715','cissp','core',2,'2.5',$$What elements should every entry in a records retention schedule define?$$,$$The record type/category (e.g., "employee tax withholding forms"), the retention period (how long to keep it), the trigger event that starts the clock (e.g., contract termination date, fiscal year end), and the disposition action to take once the period ends (secure destruction, archival, or transfer to another custodian).$$),
('ci-f-716','cissp','core',2,'2.5',$$What are the six phases of the e-discovery process?$$,$$Identify (locate potentially relevant information), Preserve (protect it from alteration/deletion, typically via legal hold), Collect (gather it in a forensically sound manner), Process (reduce volume and convert formats), Review (assess relevance and privilege), and Produce (deliver responsive material to the requesting party).$$),
('ci-f-717','cissp','core',2,'2.5',$$How does the GDPR "right to erasure" interact with a legal hold or statutory retention duty?$$,$$The right to erasure is not absolute - GDPR itself recognizes that a controller may refuse or delay erasure when retention is necessary for compliance with a legal obligation (such as an active litigation hold or a mandatory statutory retention period). The organization must document the specific lawful basis for continuing to hold the data and revisit the request once that basis expires.$$),
('ci-f-718','cissp','core',2,'2.5',$$What role does a records custodian (records manager) play in a retention program?$$,$$The records custodian administers the retention schedule day to day - tracking which records exist, when their retention periods expire, coordinating approved disposition, and maintaining the list of active legal holds so that no record under hold is accidentally destroyed on schedule.$$),
('ci-f-719','cissp','core',2,'2.5',$$Give examples of statutory minimum retention periods that commonly appear on CISSP-style scenarios.$$,$$Sarbanes-Oxley (SOX) requires certain financial audit records be retained around seven years; HIPAA requires covered entities retain required documentation for six years from creation or last effective date; U.S. tax records are generally expected to be retained around seven years to cover IRS audit windows. Exact figures vary by jurisdiction and record type - the exam tests the concept that regulation, not convenience, sets these floors.$$),
('ci-f-720','cissp','core',2,'2.5',$$Under what two conditions may a record finally be securely destroyed?$$,$$Both must be true: (1) the record's required retention period has fully expired, and (2) no legal hold, audit, or other exceptional preservation requirement currently applies to it. Meeting only the first condition is not sufficient - an expired retention period does not override an active hold.$$),
('ci-f-721','cissp','core',2,'2.5',$$How does backup retention differ from a formal records retention program?$$,$$Backups exist for operational recovery - restoring service after failure or corruption - and typically follow short rotation cycles (e.g., a grandfather-father-son scheme) driven by RPO/RTO needs, not legal requirements. They are not a substitute for records retention: backups often retain far more data than any retention schedule requires, which complicates legal hold scope (holds may need to reach into backup sets) and can itself create unintended over-retention risk.$$),
-- ---------- 2.5 asset retention: acronym ----------
('ci-a-253','cissp','acronym',2,'2.5',$$ESI$$,$$Electronically Stored Information - the legal term for digital records (emails, documents, databases, logs) subject to preservation, collection, and production obligations in e-discovery and legal holds.$$),
('ci-a-254','cissp','acronym',2,'2.5',$$FRCP$$,$$Federal Rules of Civil Procedure - the U.S. federal rules (notably Rule 26 and Rule 37) governing e-discovery obligations, including the duty to preserve electronically stored information once litigation is reasonably anticipated.$$),
('ci-a-255','cissp','acronym',2,'2.5',$$ILM$$,$$Information Lifecycle Management - the discipline of managing data's value, cost, and risk across its life from creation through retention and disposal, aligning storage and retention decisions with the data's stage and importance.$$),
-- ---------- 2.5 asset retention: feynman ----------
('ci-y-152','cissp','feynman',2,'2.5',$$Explain why an organization can get in trouble for BOTH destroying records too early and keeping them too long.$$,$$Model answer: Retention isn't a single "keep forever" or "delete right away" choice - it's a window with a floor and a ceiling. The floor comes from regulators and courts: certain financial, tax, health, or employment records have to exist for a set number of years so they can be audited, taxed, or produced in a dispute, and destroying them before that window closes is itself a violation with its own fines. The ceiling comes from risk: every record you keep past the point it's actually needed is one more thing that can be stolen in a breach, one more thing that has to be reviewed and produced if you're ever sued, and one more thing regulators like GDPR say you had no right to still be holding. So the safe zone is "keep it exactly as long as required or genuinely useful, then get rid of it" - both edges of that window carry real consequences.$$),
('ci-y-153','cissp','feynman',2,'2.5',$$Explain what a legal hold does to an organization's normal data retention schedule and why ignoring it is dangerous.$$,$$Model answer: Normally, a retention schedule runs on autopilot - once a record's clock runs out, it gets deleted automatically, often by a scheduled job nobody thinks about day to day. A legal hold interrupts that autopilot for a specific slice of data: as soon as litigation is reasonably anticipated, anything that might be relevant has to be frozen in place, no matter what the regular schedule says, until the hold is lifted. If the automated purge job isn't told about the hold and quietly deletes those records anyway, that's not a technical glitch in the eyes of a court - it's spoliation of evidence, and judges can respond by assuming the missing evidence was damaging to whoever destroyed it, or by imposing financial penalties. The danger isn't the deletion itself; it's that it happened to evidence someone had a duty to keep.$$),
('ci-y-154','cissp','feynman',2,'2.5',$$Explain the tension between GDPR's right to erasure and a company's obligation to retain financial records for audits.$$,$$Model answer: GDPR gives individuals a right to ask a company to delete their personal data, but that right was never meant to let people erase records a company is legally required to keep. If a customer's invoice data is part of financial records a tax authority requires the company to retain for several years, GDPR itself carves out an exception: the company can lawfully refuse or delay the erasure request, citing "compliance with a legal obligation" as the basis. What the company can't do is simply ignore the request - it has to document why it's refusing, limit use of that data strictly to the retention purpose, and actually delete it once the statutory retention window closes and no other justification remains. So the two rules coexist by the erasure right yielding temporarily to a specific, provable legal duty, not by one simply overriding the other.$$),
-- ---------- 2.6 data security controls & compliance: core ----------
('ci-f-722','cissp','core',2,'2.6',$$What is the difference between data sovereignty and data residency?$$,$$Data sovereignty means data is subject to the laws of the country in which it is physically stored or processed - a legal fact that applies regardless of what anyone agrees to. Data residency is a narrower, often self-imposed or contractual requirement that data physically stay within a specified location; it may reflect sovereignty concerns but doesn't carry the same independent legal force - an organization can adopt a residency requirement without it being legally mandated.$$),
('ci-f-723','cissp','core',2,'2.6',$$What is homomorphic encryption and what problem does it solve?$$,$$A form of encryption that allows computations to be performed directly on ciphertext, producing an encrypted result that, when decrypted, matches the result of performing those operations on the plaintext. It lets a third party (such as a cloud provider) process sensitive data without ever seeing it unencrypted - protecting data in use, the state that is hardest to secure with conventional controls.$$),
('ci-f-724','cissp','core',2,'2.6',$$What is a secure enclave / trusted execution environment, and how does it protect data in use?$$,$$A hardware-isolated region of a processor (e.g., Intel SGX, AMD SEV) that keeps code and data encrypted and inaccessible even to a compromised operating system, hypervisor, or a cloud provider's own privileged administrators. It underpins confidential computing by extending protection to the moment data is actively being computed on, not just while stored or in transit.$$),
('ci-f-725','cissp','core',2,'2.6',$$What are the three main DLP deployment types and where each sits?$$,$$Network DLP inspects traffic at the perimeter/egress points (email gateways, web proxies) to catch sensitive data leaving the network. Endpoint DLP runs as an agent on user devices, controlling actions like USB copy, printing, or clipboard use even when the device is offline. Storage/cloud DLP scans data at rest in file shares, databases, and cloud repositories (often via CASB integration) to find and flag sensitive data wherever it has come to reside.$$),
('ci-f-726','cissp','core',2,'2.6',$$What detection techniques do DLP tools use to identify sensitive data?$$,$$Content inspection (pattern/regex matching for things like credit card or SSN formats), exact data matching / fingerprinting (hashing known sensitive records so exact copies are recognized wherever they appear), and statistical or machine-learning classification (recognizing sensitive data by its structure or context even without an exact pattern match).$$),
('ci-f-727','cissp','core',2,'2.6',$$What legal mechanisms let an organization transfer personal data out of the EU/EEA under GDPR?$$,$$Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs) - EU Commission-approved contract terms binding the receiving party to GDPR-equivalent protections; Binding Corporate Rules (BCRs) - an internal, regulator-approved policy allowing transfers within a corporate group; and adequacy decisions - the European Commission's determination that a specific non-EU country's laws already provide adequate protection, permitting transfer without extra safeguards.$$),
('ci-f-728','cissp','core',2,'2.6',$$What does FIPS 199 impact categorization (Low/Moderate/High) determine in the control-selection process?$$,$$FIPS 199 requires categorizing a system's confidentiality, integrity, and availability impact if compromised, each as Low, Moderate, or High. The highest of the three drives which NIST SP 800-53 control baseline (Low, Moderate, or High) must be selected as the starting point - the input that scoping and tailoring then refine for the specific system.$$),
('ci-f-729','cissp','core',2,'2.6',$$What is an overlay in NIST SP 800-53, and how does it differ from tailoring a single system's baseline?$$,$$An overlay is a pre-built, specialized set of tailored controls and control refinements developed for a particular community of interest, technology, or mission need (e.g., a cloud overlay, a privacy overlay, an industrial control systems overlay) that can be applied across many systems sharing that context. Ordinary tailoring customizes one system's baseline; an overlay packages a reusable tailoring decision so it doesn't have to be reinvented system by system.$$),
('ci-f-730','cissp','core',2,'2.6',$$How do data masking, tokenization, anonymization, and pseudonymization differ as data protection controls?$$,$$Masking obscures data (e.g., showing only the last four digits) typically for non-production/test environments while the real value stays retrievable from the source. Tokenization substitutes a reference token for the sensitive value, with the real value held in a separate secure vault. Anonymization irreversibly strips identifying information so the data subject can no longer be identified even by the data holder. Pseudonymization replaces identifiers with a substitute but keeps a separately secured key that allows reversal - useful when a controlled re-identification path is still needed.$$),
-- ---------- 2.6 data security controls & compliance: acronym ----------
('ci-a-256','cissp','acronym',2,'2.6',$$CASB$$,$$Cloud Access Security Broker - a control point (proxy, API-integrated, or both) between users and cloud services that enforces DLP, encryption, and access policy for data moving to and residing in cloud applications.$$),
('ci-a-257','cissp','acronym',2,'2.6',$$SCC$$,$$Standard Contractual Clauses - European Commission-approved contract terms that legally bind a data recipient outside the EU/EEA to GDPR-equivalent protections, enabling lawful cross-border personal data transfer.$$),
('ci-a-258','cissp','acronym',2,'2.6',$$BCR$$,$$Binding Corporate Rules - an internal data-protection policy approved by EU regulators that allows a multinational corporate group to transfer personal data between its own entities across borders.$$),
('ci-a-259','cissp','acronym',2,'2.6',$$TDE$$,$$Transparent Data Encryption - database-level encryption that automatically encrypts data files at rest without requiring changes to the application, commonly used to protect databases against storage/media theft.$$),
('ci-a-260','cissp','acronym',2,'2.6',$$FIPS$$,$$Federal Information Processing Standards - U.S. government standards (e.g., FIPS 199 for impact categorization, FIPS 140 for cryptographic modules) that drive baseline security-control selection and approved cryptography.$$),
-- ---------- 2.6 data security controls & compliance: feynman ----------
('ci-y-155','cissp','feynman',2,'2.6',$$Explain how confidential computing protects data in use, using a cloud provider example.$$,$$Model answer: Normally, encryption protects data at rest (on disk) and in transit (over the network), but the moment a program needs to actually work with the data - run a calculation on it, search it, train a model on it - it has to be decrypted in memory first. That's the gap confidential computing closes. A secure enclave carves out a hardware-protected region of the processor where data can be decrypted and processed, but nothing outside that region - not the operating system, not the hypervisor, not even the cloud provider's own system administrators - can read what's inside it. So a hospital could send encrypted patient data to a cloud provider's confidential-computing instance, have it decrypted only inside the enclave to run an analysis, and get back an encrypted result, all without the cloud provider - or anyone who compromises the cloud provider's other software - ever seeing the raw data.$$),
('ci-y-156','cissp','feynman',2,'2.6',$$Explain the difference between scoping and tailoring a security baseline, and where an overlay fits in.$$,$$Model answer: Start with a full NIST control baseline - a big, generic list of controls appropriate to a system's impact level. Scoping is the first cut: you go through that list and remove controls that plainly don't apply to your system (for instance, dropping controls specific to systems processing classified data if yours doesn't). Tailoring comes next: for the controls that remain, you adjust their specific parameters and requirements to fit your actual environment and risk tolerance - lengthening a password-history parameter, or swapping in a compensating control where the exact control as written isn't feasible. An overlay is a shortcut for doing this work once and reusing it: instead of every cloud system, say, independently scoping and tailoring the same baseline the same way, someone builds a "cloud overlay" capturing that community's typical decisions, and individual systems adopt the overlay as their starting point instead of starting from the generic baseline every time.$$);

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- ---------- 2.4 data roles & lifecycle management ----------
('ci-q-700','cissp',2,'2.4','mcq',1,$q$A retail company's data governance program assigns one role to define what a "valid customer email address" looks like and to monitor that stored values remain accurate and consistent, while a separate role implements the encryption and access controls protecting that data. Which role defines the data's meaning and quality standards?$q$,$q$["Data owner","Data steward","Data custodian","System owner"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The data steward is responsible for the quality, definitions, and business context of data - exactly the "what does a valid value look like, and is it accurate" function described. The data owner is accountable for classification and access decisions; the data custodian implements the technical safeguards; the system owner is accountable for the hosting platform, not the data's meaning.$q$),
('ci-q-701','cissp',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$An IT team overwrites every sector of a solid-state drive once before reissuing it to a lower-sensitivity internal team, believing this fully sanitizes the drive. Why might sensitive data still be recoverable afterward?$q$,$q$["The SSD's wear-leveling and over-provisioning can leave old data in reallocated blocks the overwrite command never addresses","Solid-state drives cannot accept overwrite commands at all","A single overwrite pass always leaves a residual magnetic signature","The operating system caches all overwritten data permanently in RAM"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SSD controllers spread writes across cells for wear-leveling and keep spare over-provisioned space, so a logical overwrite of a file's addressable space may not touch every physical location that ever held a copy of the data. This is why NIST guidance favors cryptographic erasure over simple overwrite for flash media. SSDs do accept overwrite commands; the "magnetic signature" concern applies to platter-based HDDs, not flash; and RAM caching is unrelated to persistent data remanence on the drive.$q$),
('ci-q-702','cissp',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$A system has been categorized as having a Moderate confidentiality impact under a FIPS 199-style assessment, and its storage media is being retired from service entirely rather than reused. Per NIST SP 800-88 principles, which sanitization category is the appropriate minimum?$q$,$q$["Clear","Purge","No sanitization is required for Moderate-impact data","Encryption alone, without any sanitization action"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$NIST SP 800-88 ties the sanitization category to the confidentiality impact of the data being removed; Moderate-impact data being retired typically requires at least Purge (resistant to laboratory recovery techniques), a stronger action than Clear. "No sanitization" ignores the categorization entirely, and encryption of data already at rest does nothing to sanitize the media being retired.$q$),
('ci-q-703','cissp',2,'2.4','mcq',1,$q$Which statement correctly explains why degaussing is not an effective sanitization method for a solid-state drive?$q$,$q$["Degaussing disrupts magnetic domains, but SSDs store data electrically in flash cells with no magnetic domains to disrupt","Degaussing only works on optical media such as CDs and DVDs","SSDs are immune to any form of data sanitization","Degaussing requires an internet connection that SSDs do not support"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Degaussing works by exposing magnetic media to a strong magnetic field that scrambles the platter's magnetic domains. SSDs have no platters or magnetic domains - they store data in flash memory cells - so a degausser has no effect on them. SSDs can be sanitized, just not by this method; degaussing has nothing to do with optical media or network connectivity.$q$),
('ci-q-704','cissp',2,'2.4','mcq',3,$q$A cloud provider hosts a customer's data on storage shared across many tenants, making physical destruction of the specific disks impossible when the customer terminates service. What sanitization approach lets the provider render that customer's data permanently unrecoverable without touching other tenants' data on the same media?$q$,$q$["Cryptographic erasure - destroy the encryption key protecting only that customer's data","A single logical overwrite of the customer's allocated storage blocks","Degaussing the shared storage array","Waiting for the media's natural end-of-life replacement cycle"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$If each tenant's data was encrypted with a distinct key from the start, destroying just that key instantly renders the tenant's ciphertext unrecoverable without affecting other tenants sharing the same physical media - the standard approach for multi-tenant cloud sanitization. A logical overwrite has the same reallocation problems as with any flash media and doesn't guarantee full coverage; degaussing would destroy every tenant's data on the shared array (and doesn't work on flash media anyway); waiting for natural replacement leaves the data exposed indefinitely in the meantime.$q$),
('ci-q-705','cissp',2,'2.4','mcq',1,$q$What is the primary purpose of a certificate of destruction issued after media sanitization?$q$,$q$["It provides an auditable record of what was sanitized, by what method, when, and by whom, supporting compliance evidence","It restores any data recovered accidentally during the sanitization process","It replaces the need for a data classification policy going forward","It automatically notifies regulators that a data breach occurred"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A certificate of destruction documents the sanitization event for audit and compliance purposes - proof that due diligence was performed and by whom. It has nothing to do with recovering data (the goal is the opposite), doesn't replace classification policy, and sanitizing media is a routine disposal activity, not a breach requiring regulatory notification.$q$),
('ci-q-706','cissp',2,'2.4','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO statements that correctly pair a data lifecycle stage with the role primarily responsible for the activity performed there. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["At the Create stage, the data owner (or delegate) assigns the initial classification","At the Destroy stage, the data custodian carries out or oversees the required sanitization method","At the Share stage, the system owner personally approves every individual data transfer","At the Store stage, the data steward performs all encryption key rotation","At the Use stage, only external auditors may access the data"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1]'::jsonb,$q$Classification is set at creation by the accountable data owner, and sanitization at end of life is executed by the custodian per the owner's classification - both textbook role-to-stage pairings. System owners don't personally approve every transfer (that's a data governance/owner function, and usually policy-driven rather than per-transfer); stewards focus on data quality/context, not encryption key management (a custodian technical control); and restricting Use-stage access to only external auditors describes no standard lifecycle model.$q$),
('ci-q-707','cissp',2,'2.4','mcq',1,$q$A data governance team notices that the same customer's name is spelled three different ways across two systems, and a required "date of birth" field is blank for 15% of records. Which role is primarily responsible for correcting these issues?$q$,$q$["Data owner","Data custodian","Data steward","Data processor"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Inconsistent values and missing required fields are data quality problems - accuracy, completeness, and consistency - which fall to the data steward. The data owner sets classification and access policy rather than managing field-level quality; the custodian handles technical protection controls; the data processor acts on another party's instructions to process data, a role distinct from internal quality management.$q$),
('ci-q-708','cissp',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$After running a Purge operation on a batch of drives, a technician immediately releases them for external resale without further action. What step did the technician skip that NIST SP 800-88 practice recommends?$q$,$q$["Verification that the sanitization was actually successful before release","Re-classifying the drives as Top Secret","Reinstalling the original operating system on each drive","Notifying the original data owner that the drives were purchased new"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Sanitization operations can fail silently - bad sectors skipped, firmware misreporting success, or a script erroring out partway - so verification (sampling, vendor validation tools, or independent audit) before release is a recommended step, not an optional formality. Re-classifying, reinstalling an OS, or notifying about a "purchase" are unrelated to confirming sanitization succeeded.$q$),
('ci-q-709','cissp',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$Two identical hard drives held the same Confidential-level data. One will be reissued to another employee within the same department at the same classification level; the other is being shipped to an external electronics recycler. Why might these two drives legitimately require different sanitization categories?$q$,$q$["Because the destination after sanitization differs - staying within the same trust boundary versus leaving it entirely - even though the data's original sensitivity was identical","Because hard drives manufactured in different years always require different methods","Because only drives leaving the organization contain data subject to remanence","Because internal reissue never requires any sanitization at all"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$NIST SP 800-88's category selection depends not just on data sensitivity but on where the media is going next: reuse within the same trust boundary can often be satisfied with Clear, while media leaving the organization's control - even at the same original classification - typically warrants the stronger assurance of Purge or Destroy. Manufacturing year is irrelevant to the standard; both drives are equally subject to remanence; and internal reissue still requires at least a Clear pass, not "no action."$q$),
('ci-q-710','cissp',2,'2.4','ordering',2,$q$Order the general workflow an organization follows when sanitizing a piece of media, from first step to last.$q$,$q$["Determine the data's classification/sensitivity","Determine the media type","Select the sanitization method required under NIST SP 800-88","Perform the sanitization","Verify the sanitization was successful","Document and certify the disposition"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4,5]$q$::jsonb,$q$You first need to know how sensitive the data is and what kind of media holds it before you can select the correct 800-88 category; only then do you perform the action, verify it actually worked, and finally document/certify the outcome for audit purposes. Skipping ahead - for example sanitizing before confirming classification - risks under-protecting the data.$q$),
('ci-q-711','cissp',2,'2.4','mcq',1,$q$In a data governance structure, which statement correctly distinguishes a data owner's accountability from a system owner's accountability?$q$,$q$["The data owner is accountable for a specific dataset's classification and access decisions; the system owner is accountable for the platform hosting the data","The system owner sets data classification, while the data owner only manages hardware procurement","Both roles are accountable for identical responsibilities and are used interchangeably","The system owner is a purely technical role with no accountability to management"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$These are complementary, not overlapping, accountabilities: the data owner answers for the data itself (classification, who may access it), while the system owner answers for the platform - often hosting data belonging to several different data owners. Claiming the system owner sets data classification reverses the two roles; the roles are not interchangeable; and the system owner role does carry management accountability, just for the platform rather than the data content.$q$),
('ci-q-712','cissp',2,'2.4','mcq',3,$q$A company reclassifies a dataset from Internal to Confidential after a policy update. The drives storing it will remain in the same data center but will now be reused by a different, lower-clearance team within the same organization. What is the correct sanitization consideration?$q$,$q$["Because the media is moving to a lower-trust internal audience relative to its data's classification, a stronger sanitization category than a same-level internal Clear may be warranted","No sanitization is needed since the media never leaves the organization","Only the classification label needs updating; the underlying media requires no action","Degaussing is unnecessary since the data center's physical security is already strong"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Even though the media stays inside the organization, reuse by an audience with lower clearance than the data's classification is functionally similar to media leaving its original trust boundary - the same logic behind requiring a stronger category (Purge rather than a routine Clear) applies. Relabeling classification doesn't remove the residual data risk; "no action" ignores the trust-boundary change entirely; and physical security of the data center doesn't substitute for sanitizing the media's content.$q$),
-- ---------- 2.5 asset retention ----------
('ci-q-713','cissp',2,'2.5','mcq',1,$q$What is a legal hold (litigation hold)?$q$,$q$["A directive to preserve all records relevant to reasonably anticipated or ongoing litigation, suspending normal destruction schedules","A routine annual review of the retention schedule","A physical lock placed on a filing cabinet containing paper records","A backup taken immediately before a scheduled system upgrade"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A legal hold is triggered by reasonably anticipated or actual litigation and requires preserving potentially relevant records, overriding whatever the normal retention schedule would otherwise do (including any automated deletion). It is not a routine review, a physical security measure, or an operational backup - it's a preservation obligation tied to legal risk.$q$),
('ci-q-714','cissp',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$A company receives a formal notice that a former employee has filed a wrongful-termination lawsuit. The HR system automatically purges emails older than 12 months every night. What should the company do FIRST regarding that employee's related records?$q$,$q$["Issue a legal hold and suspend the automated purge job for records relevant to the matter","Wait until the lawsuit is formally served before taking any action","Immediately archive and encrypt all company email regardless of relevance","Delete the employee's records to avoid producing anything embarrassing"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The duty to preserve begins once litigation is "reasonably anticipated," which a filed lawsuit clearly satisfies - waiting for formal service risks records being auto-purged in the meantime and constitutes spoliation. The hold should be scoped to relevant records, not the entirety of company email, and deleting relevant records is spoliation, potentially resulting in severe sanctions.$q$),
('ci-q-715','cissp',2,'2.5','mcq',3,$q$During litigation, opposing counsel discovers that an organization's backup rotation destroyed emails known to be relevant to the case three weeks after the lawsuit was filed and the organization's own legal team had already circulated a hold notice internally. What is the MOST likely consequence the court may impose?$q$,$q$["An adverse inference instruction or other sanctions for spoliation of evidence","No consequence, since backup rotation is an automated, routine IT process","A requirement that the organization pay for opposing counsel's travel expenses only","Automatic dismissal of the lawsuit in the organization's favor"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Destroying relevant evidence after a duty to preserve has attached - here, after both the lawsuit was filed and an internal hold notice existed - is spoliation, and courts commonly respond with an adverse inference instruction (the jury may assume the lost evidence was unfavorable) or other sanctions. "Automated and routine" does not excuse the failure to configure the process around the hold; the other outcomes describe unrelated or implausible remedies.$q$),
('ci-q-716','cissp',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$A dataset's regulatory minimum retention period (7 years) has just been reached, the business unit confirms it has no further operational use for the data, and no legal hold or audit is in effect. What is the most appropriate action under sound retention practice?$q$,$q$["Proceed with the scheduled secure disposition of the data per the retention schedule","Retain the data indefinitely to be safe","Immediately notify all customers before any action is taken","Convert the data to a different format and keep it permanently as a backup"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Once the regulatory minimum has been met, there is no ongoing business need, and no hold applies, the retention schedule's disposition action should proceed - keeping data past its justified need only adds breach and discovery exposure with no benefit. Indefinite retention and unnecessary permanent backups both violate storage-limitation principles; a general customer notification is not a standard or required step for routine, policy-driven disposal.$q$),
('ci-q-717','cissp',2,'2.5','mcq',1,$q$What is the primary purpose of a records retention schedule?$q$,$q$["To specify how long each category of record must be kept and what disposition action follows","To list every employee authorized to view sensitive records","To define the encryption algorithm used for archived data","To calculate the Recovery Time Objective for IT systems"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A retention schedule is the governing document translating legal, regulatory, and business requirements into concrete rules for how long each record type must be kept and what happens to it afterward. It is not an access-control list, a cryptography standard, or a disaster-recovery metric, though it can inform related policies.$q$),
('ci-q-718','cissp',2,'2.5','multi',2,$q$Select the FOUR elements that a well-formed retention schedule entry should define for a given record category. (Choose four.)$q$,$q$["The record type or category","The retention period","The trigger event that starts the retention clock","The disposition action once the period ends","The specific encryption algorithm the record must use"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2,3]'::jsonb,$q$A retention schedule entry needs to identify what the record is, how long to keep it, what event starts the countdown (e.g., contract end date), and what happens when the period ends (destroy, archive, or transfer). The specific encryption algorithm is a data-security control decision, not a retention-schedule element - it belongs to a separate security standard, not the retention policy itself.$q$),
('ci-q-719','cissp',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$An organization keeps a customer's purchase history for eleven years after its documented six-year regulatory retention requirement expired, with no legal hold or ongoing business justification. What is the PRIMARY risk this practice creates?$q$,$q$["Expanded breach liability and legal/regulatory exposure from holding data with no remaining lawful purpose","Reduced cost, since older data is cheaper to store than new data","Improved data quality, since more historical data is always better","Automatic compliance with data minimization requirements"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Data held with no active purpose past its required retention period increases both the volume of sensitive data exposed in a breach and legal exposure (it remains discoverable and is unjustifiable under storage-limitation principles). It does not reduce cost meaningfully relative to the risk, does not itself improve data quality, and is the opposite of data-minimization compliance.$q$),
('ci-q-720','cissp',2,'2.5','ordering',2,$q$Order the e-discovery process phases from first to last.$q$,$q$["Identify","Preserve","Collect","Process","Review","Produce"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4,5]$q$::jsonb,$q$E-discovery proceeds by first identifying potentially relevant information, preserving it (typically via legal hold) so it isn't lost, collecting it in a forensically sound way, processing it to reduce volume and normalize formats, reviewing it for relevance and privilege, and finally producing the responsive material to the requesting party.$q$),
('ci-q-721','cissp',2,'2.5','mcq',3,$q$A data subject submits a GDPR erasure request for personal data that is also part of financial records under an active litigation hold related to a regulatory investigation. What is the organization's correct response?$q$,$q$["Decline or delay the erasure, documenting the legal-obligation basis, and revisit the request once the hold and any statutory retention requirement no longer apply","Immediately erase the data to comply with the request, since GDPR erasure rights always override litigation holds","Ignore the request entirely without any response to the data subject","Erase only the portions of the data not directly related to the litigation, regardless of the records' integrity"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$GDPR's right to erasure is not absolute - it explicitly yields when retention is necessary for compliance with a legal obligation, such as an active hold or mandatory retention duty - but the organization must document that basis and respond to the request rather than ignore it, revisiting erasure once the obligation lapses. Immediately erasing would itself risk spoliation and regulatory violation; silently ignoring the request is itself a compliance failure; and selectively erasing only "unrelated" portions risks destroying record integrity needed for the litigation.$q$),
('ci-q-722','cissp',2,'2.5','mcq',1,$q$What is the primary responsibility of a records custodian (records manager) within a retention program?$q$,$q$["Administering the retention schedule day to day - tracking expirations, coordinating disposition, and maintaining the list of active legal holds","Writing the organization's information security policy from scratch","Approving all capital expenditure related to IT infrastructure","Conducting penetration tests against production systems"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The records custodian's job is operational administration of the retention program itself: knowing what records exist, when they expire, coordinating approved disposal, and making sure nothing under an active legal hold is accidentally destroyed. Policy authorship, capital budgeting, and penetration testing are separate functions belonging to other roles.$q$),
('ci-q-723','cissp',2,'2.5','mcq',1,$q$Under a records retention program, what is the correct relationship between a regulatory minimum retention period and destruction of a record?$q$,$q$["A record generally may not be destroyed before its regulatory minimum retention period has elapsed","A record must always be destroyed the day its minimum retention period elapses, with no exceptions","Regulatory minimums apply only to paper records, never to electronic records","Regulatory minimums are advisory only and carry no compliance consequence"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The regulatory minimum is a floor - destroying a record before that period elapses is itself a compliance violation, independent of any other requirement. The record doesn't have to be destroyed the instant the minimum passes (a legal hold or other justification can extend retention further); regulatory minimums commonly apply to electronic records too; and these minimums typically carry real fines or sanctions, not merely advisory weight.$q$),
('ci-q-724','cissp',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$An HR department retains former employees' performance review records for fifteen years after the applicable regulatory and contractual retention requirement of five years has passed, citing no specific ongoing need. Under sound retention practice, what is the main concern with this?$q$,$q$["The extended retention increases the scope and impact of a potential data breach and legal discovery without any offsetting requirement or benefit","There is no concern, since HR records are never subject to any retention limits","The records become more accurate the longer they are retained","Longer retention automatically satisfies GDPR's data minimization principle"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Retaining sensitive HR data far beyond its required period, with no justified business need, needlessly expands what could be exposed in a breach and what must be reviewed/produced in litigation or regulatory inquiry - exactly the risk retention schedules are meant to control. HR records are absolutely subject to retention limits; retention length has no bearing on data accuracy; and over-retention is the opposite of satisfying data-minimization principles.$q$),
('ci-q-725','cissp',2,'2.5','matching',2,$q$Match each record type to the requirement or driver that most directly shapes its typical retention period.$q$,$q${"left":["Financial audit records","Protected health information","Signed commercial contracts","Federal tax filings and support"],"right":["Sarbanes-Oxley (SOX) financial reporting requirements","HIPAA documentation retention requirements","The applicable statute of limitations for contract disputes","IRS recordkeeping requirements for tax audit windows"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$SOX drives retention of financial audit records; HIPAA sets documentation retention obligations for protected health information; the statute of limitations for breach-of-contract claims typically drives how long signed contracts are kept; and IRS rules drive how long tax records and their supporting documentation must be retained to survive a potential audit.$q$),
('ci-q-726','cissp',2,'2.5','mcq',3,$q$A company under an active legal hold discovers that the relevant records also exist across several rolling backup tapes that are normally overwritten every 90 days as part of the standard backup rotation. What consideration does this raise for the legal hold?$q$,$q$["The hold's preservation scope must extend to relevant copies within the backup rotation, requiring the automated overwrite cycle to be suspended for those specific backup sets","Backup tapes are exempt from legal holds because they are not part of the primary production system","The legal hold only applies to the most recent backup, regardless of where relevant data resides","Backup rotation should continue unchanged since litigation holds apply only to live/active data"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A legal hold's preservation duty extends to all reasonably relevant copies of the data, not just the live production copy - if relevant information only exists on backup media due to the normal rotation, that rotation cycle must be paused for the affected backup sets to avoid overwriting responsive data. Backups are not categorically exempt from holds, the hold isn't limited to only the newest backup, and continuing rotation unchanged risks the same spoliation problem discussed elsewhere in this domain.$q$),
('ci-q-727','cissp',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$How does a documented, consistently-applied retention and disposition program (including certificates of destruction) help an organization if it is later accused of improperly destroying evidence?$q$,$q$["It provides evidence that the destruction occurred as part of routine, good-faith policy rather than as an attempt to conceal relevant material","It guarantees the organization cannot be sanctioned under any circumstances","It replaces the need to issue legal holds once litigation is anticipated","It allows the organization to destroy records under an active legal hold without consequence"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Courts generally distinguish between destruction that follows a documented, routinely applied retention policy (evidence of good faith) and destruction that appears selective or convenient once litigation loomed - a consistent program with disposal records is a key defense against spoliation claims. It doesn't guarantee immunity from sanctions, doesn't eliminate the separate duty to issue holds once litigation is anticipated, and never authorizes destroying records that are actually under an active hold.$q$),
-- ---------- 2.6 data security controls & compliance ----------
('ci-q-728','cissp',2,'2.6','mcq',1,$q$What is the primary control category used to protect data at rest, such as files on a database server's storage volumes?$q$,$q$["Encryption (e.g., full-disk or transparent database encryption)","Multi-factor authentication for every file read","Perfect forward secrecy key exchange","Data loss prevention endpoint agents alone"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Data at rest is primarily protected through encryption applied to the storage medium or database (full-disk encryption, transparent data encryption), which renders the data unintelligible if the underlying media is stolen or accessed without authorization. MFA governs who can log in, not the confidentiality of stored bytes; perfect forward secrecy is a data-in-transit key-exchange property; and DLP alone detects/blocks movement rather than protecting data that never moves.$q$),
('ci-q-729','cissp',2,'2.6','mcq',2,$q$A financial analytics vendor wants to run statistical computations on a client's sensitive dataset without the vendor ever being able to view the underlying values, even temporarily during processing. Which technology is designed for exactly this requirement?$q$,$q$["Homomorphic encryption, allowing computation directly on encrypted data","Transparent Data Encryption applied to the database at rest","A standard TLS-encrypted API connection to transfer the data","A hashed one-way digest of the sensitive values"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Homomorphic encryption allows mathematical operations to be performed directly on ciphertext, producing an encrypted result that decrypts to the same answer as if the operations had been run on the plaintext - meaning the vendor never needs the underlying values in the clear at any point. TDE protects storage, not computation; TLS protects the data only while moving between endpoints, not while being processed; and a one-way hash cannot be reversed to yield a usable computation result at all.$q$),
('ci-q-730','cissp',2,'2.6','mcq',2,$q$A healthcare research consortium trains a machine-learning model on pooled patient data hosted in a multi-tenant cloud environment, and wants assurance that neither the cloud provider's system administrators nor a compromised hypervisor can inspect the raw patient data while the model is actively training on it. Which capability provides this assurance?$q$,$q$["A hardware-based secure enclave / trusted execution environment isolating the training process from the host system","Standard database-level Transparent Data Encryption","IPsec tunneling between the data source and the training cluster","Role-based access control on the training cluster's file share"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A secure enclave (trusted execution environment) isolates code and data in a hardware-protected region of the processor that remains inaccessible even to a compromised hypervisor or privileged cloud administrator - this is what protects data in use during active computation like model training. TDE protects data at rest, IPsec protects data in transit, and RBAC on a file share controls access to stored files rather than protecting data actively being processed in memory.$q$),
('ci-q-731','cissp',2,'2.6','mcq',1,$q$An organization wants to prevent employees from copying sensitive files to USB drives even when their laptops are disconnected from the corporate network. Which DLP deployment type is designed for this scenario?$q$,$q$["Endpoint DLP","Network DLP","Storage/cloud DLP","Perimeter firewall DLP rules"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Endpoint DLP runs as an agent directly on the device, so it can enforce policy (like blocking USB transfers or clipboard actions) even when the device has no network connectivity. Network DLP only inspects traffic actually crossing the network perimeter, so it can't see or block an offline USB copy; storage/cloud DLP scans data already at rest in repositories, not device-level actions; and there is no standard "perimeter firewall DLP" category distinct from network DLP.$q$),
('ci-q-732','cissp',2,'2.6','multi',2,$q$Select the THREE detection techniques a DLP solution commonly uses to identify sensitive data. (Choose three.)$q$,$q$["Content inspection via pattern/regex matching","Exact data matching / fingerprinting against known sensitive records","Statistical or machine-learning classification of data structure and context","Signature-based antivirus scanning for known malware hashes","Physical badge-reader logs at building entrances"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2]'::jsonb,$q$DLP tools commonly rely on content inspection (regex/pattern matching for formats like credit card numbers), fingerprinting (hashing known sensitive records to recognize exact copies), and statistical/ML classification (recognizing sensitive data by structure or context). Signature-based antivirus and physical badge logs are unrelated technologies addressing different problems (malware detection and physical access, respectively), not data-loss-prevention techniques.$q$),
('ci-q-733','cissp',2,'2.6','mcq',3,$q$A U.S.-based SaaS vendor wants to process personal data of EU customers on servers located in the United States, and no European Commission adequacy decision covers the vendor's processing arrangement. Which mechanism can the vendor use to lawfully enable this cross-border transfer?$q$,$q$["Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs) between the EU data exporter and the U.S. vendor","A self-declared internal privacy policy published on the vendor's website","Storing the data temporarily in the EU before immediately re-transferring it, which removes any transfer obligation","Relying on the customer's general acceptance of the vendor's terms of service alone"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$In the absence of an adequacy decision, Standard Contractual Clauses are the standard GDPR-recognized mechanism binding the receiving party outside the EU/EEA to GDPR-equivalent protections, enabling a lawful transfer. A self-published privacy policy carries no independent legal weight for this purpose, briefly routing data through the EU does not eliminate the cross-border transfer obligation, and generic terms-of-service acceptance does not satisfy GDPR's specific transfer-mechanism requirements.$q$),
('ci-q-734','cissp',2,'2.6','mcq',1,$q$Which statement correctly distinguishes data sovereignty from data residency?$q$,$q$["Data sovereignty is a legal fact based on the laws of the country where data is stored, while data residency is a location requirement that may be self-imposed or contractual rather than independently mandated by law","Data residency is always a stricter legal requirement than data sovereignty","The two terms are fully interchangeable with no meaningful difference","Data sovereignty applies only to government data, never to commercial data"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Sovereignty means the data is legally subject to the laws of wherever it physically sits, regardless of anyone's preference; residency is a narrower requirement - often set by policy or contract - that data stay in a specific location, which may or may not correspond to an actual legal mandate. Residency is not inherently "stricter" than sovereignty (sovereignty is the legal reality residency requirements often try to manage); the terms are related but distinct; and sovereignty applies to any data physically located in a jurisdiction, government or commercial.$q$),
('ci-q-735','cissp',2,'2.6','mcq',2,$q$A new system is categorized under a FIPS 199-style assessment as Low for confidentiality, Moderate for integrity, and High for availability. Which impact level should drive the initial NIST SP 800-53 control baseline selected for the system?$q$,$q$["High","Low","Moderate","The average of the three ratings, rounded down"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Under FIPS 199 categorization, the overall system impact level - and therefore the starting control baseline - is set to the HIGHEST of the confidentiality, integrity, and availability ratings, since the system must be protected to the level of its most sensitive property. Selecting Low or Moderate would under-protect the High-availability requirement, and there is no standard practice of averaging the three ratings.$q$),
('ci-q-736','cissp',2,'2.6','mcq',2,$q$An organization repeatedly reinvents the same set of control adjustments every time it deploys a new cloud-hosted system, because each project team tailors the generic NIST baseline independently. What NIST SP 800-53 concept is designed to eliminate this duplicated effort?$q$,$q$["An overlay - a reusable, pre-built set of tailored controls for a specific community or technology like cloud","A completely new, custom control catalog written from scratch for each project","Removing the requirement to categorize the system under FIPS 199","Applying the High baseline to every system regardless of its actual impact level"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An overlay packages a community's typical scoping and tailoring decisions (e.g., a cloud overlay) into a reusable starting point, so individual project teams don't have to independently re-derive the same adjustments to the generic baseline. Writing an entirely new catalog per project is the wasteful behavior the overlay concept is meant to prevent; skipping FIPS 199 categorization removes the very input the baseline selection depends on; and blanket-applying the High baseline everywhere over-controls lower-impact systems unnecessarily.$q$),
('ci-q-737','cissp',2,'2.6','mcq',2,$q$A payment processor replaces a customer's actual credit card number with a randomly generated reference value everywhere it is used in downstream systems, while the real card number is stored only in a separate, tightly controlled vault. What technique is this?$q$,$q$["Tokenization","Data masking","Anonymization","Hashing with a fixed salt"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Tokenization substitutes a sensitive value with a reference token that has no exploitable mathematical relationship to the original, while the real value is kept separately in a secure vault - exactly this scenario. Masking typically just obscures part of a value for display/non-production use without a separate vault-and-lookup design; anonymization irreversibly removes the ability to recover the original value at all (the opposite of what a payment processor needs, since it must be able to charge the real card); and a fixed salted hash is not the standard mechanism used for reversible payment tokenization.$q$),
('ci-q-738','cissp',2,'2.6','ordering',2,$q$Order the general steps an organization follows when selecting a compliant cloud storage region for regulated data subject to data sovereignty requirements.$q$,$q$["Classify the data and determine which jurisdictions' laws apply to it","Identify cloud regions that are permissible under those jurisdictional requirements","Select a specific provider and deployment region meeting the requirement","Implement contractual transfer safeguards where data must still cross borders (e.g., SCCs)","Monitor and periodically audit for ongoing compliance"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$You first have to know what the data is and which laws govern it before you can even identify which regions are permissible; from that shortlist you choose a specific provider/region, add any needed contractual safeguards for whatever cross-border movement remains, and then monitor on an ongoing basis, since cloud configurations and legal requirements can both drift over time.$q$),
('ci-q-739','cissp',2,'2.6','matching',2,$q$Match each data-security control or technique to the data state or condition it is primarily designed to protect.$q$,$q${"left":["Transparent Data Encryption on a database","TLS encryption on an API connection","A hardware secure enclave protecting a live computation","A CASB scanning files stored in a SaaS application"],"right":["Protects data at rest inside the database","Protects data in transit across the network","Protects data in use during active processing","Protects data at rest within a cloud application"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$TDE protects the database's stored files (at rest); TLS protects data as it moves across the network (in transit); a secure enclave protects data actively being computed on (in use); and a CASB scanning SaaS storage protects data sitting at rest within a cloud application, extending at-rest protection into environments the organization doesn't directly control.$q$),
('ci-q-740','cissp',2,'2.6','mcq',3,$q$A retailer accepts credit card payments across dozens of systems but redesigns its network so that only a small, isolated segment of servers actually stores, processes, or transmits cardholder data, removing that responsibility from every other system. Under PCI DSS, what security-baseline concept does this network redesign illustrate?$q$,$q$["Scoping - reducing which systems fall within the compliance boundary (the cardholder data environment) that the full control baseline must be applied to","Tailoring - customizing individual control parameters for each system","Data sovereignty - ensuring cardholder data stays within a specific country","An overlay - a prebuilt set of controls specific to the retail industry"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Segmenting cardholder data into an isolated environment and thereby removing the rest of the network from the compliance boundary is a real-world example of scoping - determining which systems the full control set even applies to - which shrinks both audit burden and risk exposure. Tailoring would customize controls for systems still in scope rather than removing systems from scope entirely; this scenario has no jurisdictional/cross-border element (sovereignty); and it is not a pre-packaged overlay, though overlays could be built for a PCI-style scope in principle.$q$),
('ci-q-741','cissp',2,'2.6','mcq',2,$q$An organization needs to let approved researchers re-identify specific study participants under controlled conditions if a significant new health finding requires follow-up, while protecting participant identities during the rest of the study. Which technique best satisfies this requirement, as opposed to fully anonymizing the data?$q$,$q$["Pseudonymization - replacing identifiers with a substitute while keeping a separately secured key that allows authorized re-identification","Anonymization - permanently and irreversibly removing all identifying information","Data masking for a non-production test environment","Tokenization of only the participants' payment information"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Pseudonymization is specifically designed for cases where a controlled, authorized path back to the individual's identity must remain possible - the substitute identifier can be reversed using a securely held key. Anonymization is irreversible by design, which would prevent exactly the follow-up re-identification the researchers need; masking is intended for non-production/test data display, not a live research re-identification workflow; and tokenizing only payment information doesn't address protecting or reversing participant identity data at all.$q$);
